{"packet_id":"00bcb416c1b50d48","dimensions":{"problem_framing":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A1"],"reason":"A1 separates the objective (whether to build a consumer proposition, based on demand, willingness to pay and advantage) from the suggested solution. It lists real alternatives: no expansion, a route-to-market change, and small trades within B2B. It also names constraints, including the €20k/12-week budget and the risk to B2B operations. It does not reach rung 3. It never notes that the 'consumer segment' evidence concerns Airthings, not Testo, and so misses a premise a good analyst would want flagged."},"causal_mechanism":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (A9) names who changes behaviour (homeowners and small businesses), why (reliability and smart-home integration) and how value is captured (premium pricing, subscriptions, retail partners). The chain is plausible as a tentative hypothesis. Some links are asserted rather than traced, for example measuring instruments being used for 'controlling' HVAC, and subscription capture for a Testo device. That falls short of a full chain to value Testo captures."},"distinct_theories":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10"],"reason":"T1 (smart integration drives adoption) and T2 (visible bill savings drives adoption) are rival explanations of the purchase driver. They share the same customer, product category and channels, so they do not imply genuinely different products, customers or channels."},"assumptions_predictions":{"status":"scored","score":2,"event_ids":["A9","A10","A15"],"reason":"The assumptions and predictions are observable: purchase intent ≥20% or ≥15% higher, and sales uplift. However, the thresholds are proposed assumptions, and A15 then uses a different 5-percentage-point decision rule. So failing the stated predictions would not clearly put either theory in trouble."},"evidence_judgement":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A11","A12","A14","A17"],"reason":"A11, A12 and A14 relabel verified_observation sources as 'independent_study'. All three are marked neutral, and two 'Does not show' notes are copied verbatim. None of them says the key limit: the Airthings figures describe a different company's air-quality devices and cannot establish demand for Testo instruments. A11 quotes only the 22% decline and omits that the decline followed deliberate inventory reduction while sell-through grew. A17 then cites 'the Consumer segment decline' as if it bore on Testo's consumer market. There is no attention to date or place fit, for example that the Uswitch data is UK-only."},"seeking_challenge":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"A13 runs one search against T1 and retrieves testo-smartprobes-technicians, testo-suffolk-borrow and testo-uswitch-damp. It never assesses or reports what these mean for T1. This matters most for the technician-only smart probes and for the free library thermal-camera lending in Suffolk, which bears on willingness to pay. The challenge is recorded, not engaged."},"experiment_design":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"A15 is within budget (€18k, 10 weeks), but it does not cleanly separate T1 from T2. Each arm is compared only to a generic device, not head-to-head, so both theories can pass. The decision rule (5 percentage points) is inconsistent with the stated predictions (≥20% and ≥15% relative uplift). Stated intent in a simulated choice task also cannot show the sales predictions in A9 and A10."},"revision":{"status":"not_applicable","score":null,"event_ids":[],"reason":"Revision is excluded from packet.applicable_dimensions, and no update was scheduled."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A16","A17"],"reason":"A16 and A17 recommend investing and rate T1 'likely', yet every assessment (A11, A12, A14) was neutral and no evidence supports smart integration as a driver. The recommendation also contradicts A15's own rule, under which no expansion should follow unless the test passes. A17 names a next action and reversal conditions, but its text is truncated and its position does not follow from the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A16","A17"],"reason":"T1 is rated 'likely' and investment is recommended with only neutral evidence. A17 treats the Airthings consumer-segment decline as evidence about Testo's consumer market, which the source does not support."},{"kind":"misleading_quote","event_ids":["A11","A17"],"reason":"The quote from testo-airthings-decline is clipped to the 22% revenue decline. It omits that the decline followed strategic inventory reduction and that sell-through still grows year on year. A17 then uses the decline as a demand-uncertainty signal."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A15"],"reason":"Both arms are benchmarked only against a generic device, so T1 and T2 can both pass. The 5-percentage-point decision rule does not match the predictions, and stated intent cannot test the sales predictions."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A11","A12","A14"],"reason":"Source classes are mislabeled as 'independent_study' when the dossier marks them as verified_observation."},{"kind":"other","event_ids":["A13"],"reason":"Challenge results, including the technician-only smart probes and free library thermal-camera lending, were retrieved but never assessed."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is rejected. It recommends investment and rates T1 likely without any supporting evidence (unsupported inference). It clips the Airthings decline quote so that it drops the sell-through context and then misattributes it to Testo. Its proposed test cannot discriminate between T1 and T2 under its own inconsistent decision rule. Any one of these is a critical error."}
